	As near as I can determine, "dweomer" comes from Old English
"dwimor", meaning a phantom, illusion, or apparition. I suspect that
it's related to OE "dwinan", meaning to waste away, fade, or disappear
(from which we get "dwindle")... a "dwimor" being something that
"dwineð".

	I think "DWIM" is just an acronym, modelled on assembly-language
mnemonics (like the Z80's "DJNZ", "Decrement and Jump if Non-Zero"), and
any similarities between a dwimor and a dwimmer are just coincidence.

